\title{Viscosity and Intermolecular Forces}
\summary{Build a falling-ball viscometer from a ballpoint pen ball and a graduated cylinder, compare four liquids, and determine the activation energy of viscous flow for glycerin from its viscosity at five temperatures.}
\tags{viscosity, intermolecular forces, Arrhenius, Stokes' law, glycerin}
\minutes{170}

Viscosity is the part of a fluid's resistance to flow that comes from intermolecular attractions. Because a molecule must pull away from its neighbors to flow, the way viscosity falls with temperature reveals how strong those attractions are. In this lab you build a viscometer from a ballpoint pen ball and a beaker of glycerin, and measure glycerin at four temperatures to find the strength of the attractions between its molecules.

\info[label="For the curious: viscous and inertial drag"]{
A fluid resists a moving object in two ways. **Viscous drag** comes from layers of fluid shearing past one another near the object's surface; it is proportional to the object's speed and to the viscosity, and it is what Stokes' law describes. **Inertial drag** comes from the object having to push fluid out of its way and leave a wake behind it; it is proportional to the fluid's density and to the square of the speed, and it does not depend on viscosity at all. Which one dominates is set by the Reynolds number, $\mathrm{Re} = \rho v d / \eta$, the ratio of inertial to viscous forces. A 1 mm ball creeping through glycerin has $\mathrm{Re}$ well below 1, so viscous drag is essentially the whole story and the fall time measures viscosity. The same ball in water has $\mathrm{Re}$ in the hundreds: inertial drag dominates, the fall time says almost nothing about viscosity, and Stokes' law does not apply.
}

# Equations

Stokes' law for a small sphere falling at terminal velocity $v$:

$$\eta = \frac{2 r^{2} (\rho_{\mathrm{ball}} - \rho_{\mathrm{liquid}}) g}{9 v}$$

where $\eta$ is the viscosity (Pa·s), $r$ the ball radius (m), $\rho_{\mathrm{ball}}$ and $\rho_{\mathrm{liquid}}$ the densities (kg/m³), $g = 9.81$ m/s², and $v$ the terminal velocity (m/s).

Arrhenius equation for viscosity:

$$\eta = \eta_{0} e^{E_{a}/RT} \quad\Rightarrow\quad \ln \eta = \ln \eta_{0} + \frac{E_{a}}{R} \cdot \frac{1}{T}$$

where $E_{a}$ is the activation energy of viscous flow (J/mol), $R = 8.314$ J/(mol·K), $T$ the absolute temperature (K), and $\eta_{0}$ a constant. A plot of $\ln \eta$ against $1/T$ is a straight line of slope $E_{a}/R$.

# Materials

**Chemicals**

- [ ] Glycerin, 99% or higher, from a pharmacy: about 300 mL (two 6 oz bottles). Vegetable glycerin sold for skin care is the same product.

**Equipment**

- [ ] BIC Cristal or Round Stic medium ballpoint pen (one)
- [ ] Small strong magnet (a neodymium magnet, or a refrigerator magnet)
- [ ] 250 mL glass beaker (inside diameter about 65 mm)
- [ ] Wire cutter, diagonal cutter, flush cutter, combination pliers, multitool, or garden clipper (scissors as a last resort)
- [ ] Ziplock bag
- [ ] Paper towels
- [ ] Permanent marker
- [ ] Ruler, mm graduations
- [ ] Thermometer, −10 °C to 60 °C
- [ ] Stopwatch (a phone is fine)
- [ ] Pot or container for a water bath, wide and deep enough to hold the beaker
- [ ] Hot tap water (or a kettle); a few ice cubes for checking the thermometer

# Safety

\safety{
Wear goggles while cutting the pen tip; the brass sleeve can split and eject small fragments. The warmest water bath is only about 35 °C; if you use a kettle, handle the hot water with care and add it to the bath slowly. Spilled glycerin is slippery on hard floors; wipe spills at once. Glycerin is food-grade and is not hazardous to skin.
}

# Preparation

**0.1** This is the only tricky step in this lab. We will be cutting the very tip of a BIC pen off to extract the ball in the tip of the pen. It's possible you will find this step annoying, but after a lot of experimentation this is the easiest and most repeatable way to get the small ball needed for this lab. If you have a wire cutter, diagonal cutter, flush cutter, combination pliers, multitool, garden clipper, or similar, then this will be quicker and less tedious. If you have nothing else, use scissors, but there is some risk of damaging the edge of your scissors.

Use a medium point BIC Round Stic or BIC Cristal. Hold the body of the pen, keeping your fingers away from the tip where you will cut the pen. Cut the pen at the very tip right behind the ball. The tip will snap off, so you may want to cut inside of a ziplock bag to contain the fragments. Cutting inside of a ziplock ensures you don't lose the ball. If you made the cut perfectly the ball will have fallen out of the brass pen tip. If you cut slightly too far from the tip the brass may stay attached to the pen tip. If the brass is still attached you can either try to break it off, which is tedious, or grab a new pen, which costs 20 cents. Wipe the ball and park it on the magnet. The ball is cemented tungsten carbide (tungsten carbide grains in a cobalt binder, which is why a magnet attracts it), diameter **1.00 mm** (manufacturing tolerance is generally within 0.02 mm), density **14.7 g/cm³**.

\figure[size=small]{labs/l-1789837730423/1791397125133-abrup-screenshot-2026-10-07-at-2-18-27-pm.webp}

\figure[size=small]{labs/l-1789837730423/1791397273543-m86fv-screenshot-2026-10-07-at-2-21-03-pm.webp}

\figure[size=small]{labs/l-1789837730423/1791396239279-57ka4-1000014515.webp}

**0.2** Measure the inside diameter of the beaker with the ruler, to the nearest 1 mm. Record **D**: \input[id=d, unit=mm]

**0.3** With the permanent marker and the ruler, draw two horizontal lines around the outside of the beaker exactly **50 mm** apart, with the lower line at least 10 mm above the inside bottom of the beaker. The upper line is the upper mark and the lower line is the lower mark.

**0.4** Check the thermometer: stir it in a cup of ice and water for one minute and record the reading. It should read 0.0 °C; if it does not, record the difference and subtract it from every temperature you measure in this lab. Ice-water reading: \input[id=ice-check, unit=°C]

**0.5** Fill the beaker with glycerin to at least 15 mm above the upper mark, pouring slowly down the inside wall. Wait until no bubbles are rising past the marks. If you pour quickly there will be lots of bubbles and you will need to wait longer. Stand the beaker on a level surface. 

# Glycerin at four temperatures

Temperature is everything in this lab. Viscosity changes by several percent for each degree, so a temperature error of even 1 °C badly hurts every result. Glycerin also warms and cools slowly and unevenly: the part near the wall of the beaker follows its surroundings long before the center does, and a thermometer touching the wall reads the surroundings, not the glycerin.


**1.1** **Refrigerator.** Place the filled beaker in the refrigerator for at least 30 minutes. Take it out, immediately carry out steps 1.2 to 1.4, and enter the results in the refrigerator row.

**1.2** **Measuring the temperature.** Hold the thermometer tip in the middle of the glycerin, halfway between the two marks and not touching the beaker. Stir slowly with the thermometer until the reading stops changing, wait a few seconds for the glycerin to become still, and record **T** to 0.1 °C. Remove the thermometer.

**1.3** **Timing the fall.** Drop the ball into the glycerin from just above the surface, aiming for the center of the beaker. Position yourself so that the upper mark is exactly at eye level as the ball crosses it and start the stopwatch; then move so that the lower mark is exactly at eye level as the ball crosses it and stop the stopwatch. In a wide beaker a viewing angle of a few degrees moves the apparent crossing point by several millimeters, so eye level matters. A little drift does not matter in a wide beaker (see the wall correction curve, Figure 2); discard the run only if the ball ends up within about 10 mm of the wall. Record **t** to 0.1 s. For maximum accuracy, video the fall with the camera lens level with the marks and read the two crossing times from the video's timestamps instead of using the stopwatch.

**1.4** **Second run.** Retrieve the ball: hold the magnet against the outside of the beaker at the level of the ball and slide it up the wall to the surface, where the ball can be lifted off with a fingertip. Wipe the ball and repeat step 1.3 for a second run. Enter the temperature and both times in the data table.


**1.5** **Cool bath.** Stand the beaker in the water bath and fill the bath with cool tap water to the level of the glycerin surface. Leave it, with the thermometer suspended in the glycerin, for at least 20 minutes and until the reading has not changed for 2 minutes, adding cool water to the bath as needed to keep it near the glycerin temperature. Carry out steps 1.2 to 1.4 with the beaker still in the bath and enter the results in the cool bath row.

**1.6** **Room temperature.** Take the beaker out of the bath, dry the outside, and leave it on the bench with the thermometer suspended in the glycerin for at least 20 minutes and until the reading has not changed for 5 minutes. Carry out steps 1.2 to 1.4 and enter the results in the room temperature row.

**1.7** **Warm bath.** Stand the beaker in the water bath and fill the bath with warm tap water at about 35 °C. Leave it as in step 1.6, until the glycerin reading has not changed for 2 minutes. Carry out steps 1.2 to 1.4 with the beaker still in the bath and enter the results in the warm bath row. The fall takes only a few seconds at this temperature; be ready on the stopwatch before releasing the ball.

**1.8** Return the glycerin to its bottles, park the ball on the magnet, and wash everything with warm water.

## Accepted viscosity of pure glycerin

\figure[caption="Read the accepted viscosity at your measured temperature from the curve. Dots are the reference measurements of Segur and Oberstar (1951); the curve is the Cheng (2008) fit to them."]{labs/l-1789837730423/1791395815006-hj72b-glycerol-viscosity.png}

\info[label="For the curious: supercooled glycerin"]{
Pure glycerin melts at 17.8 °C, so the refrigerator and cool bath runs are below its freezing point. Glycerin almost never crystallizes on its own; it remains a supercooled liquid whose viscosity keeps rising smoothly as it cools, and the part of the curve below 17.8 °C refers to this supercooled liquid. If a bottle ever did freeze it would be an obvious translucent solid.
}

\info[label="Note on the container wall"]{
The correction is small for a wide beaker and large for a narrow cylinder, and the steepness of the curve at small diameters is why narrow containers also give scattered results: a ball that wanders a millimeter off the axis in a 16 mm cylinder sees a noticeably different drag than a centered one, while in a 65 mm beaker the same wander changes almost nothing. The correction is the same at every temperature, so it does not affect the activation energy.
}

\notes[id=analysis-notes]{Analysis notes}

