## Calculations and Analysis

\answer[id=calculations, lines=6]{Now we use all of our data to calculate the viscosity of glycerin at each temperature. Find the terminal velocity of the ball at each temperature. Then find the net downward force of the ball using the volume of the ball, the density of the ball ($14.7 g$/$cm^3$), and the density of glycerin ($1.26 g$/$cm^3$). Plug those numbers into Stokes law (below) to obtain the viscosity of glycerin. 

Stokes law assumes that the ball was dropped in a vast uniform ocean of glycerin. Because our beaker has a finite radius the walls of the beaker slightly slow the ball down. In a small graduated cylinder they would have slowed the ball down much more. Below there is a graph of the wall correction factor, the factor you need to divide the measured viscosity by to get the correct viscosity.}

Stokes' law for a small sphere falling at terminal velocity $v$:

$$\eta = \frac{2 r^{2} (\rho_{\mathrm{ball}} - \rho_{\mathrm{liquid}}) g}{9 v}$$

where $\eta$ is the viscosity (Pa·s), $r$ the ball radius (m), $\rho_{\mathrm{ball}}$ and $\rho_{\mathrm{liquid}}$ the densities (kg/m³), $g = 9.81$ m/s², and $v$ the terminal velocity (m/s).

\figure{labs/l-1789837730423/1791408623685-qfy0e-wall-correction.png}

